  "prediction": "**War-Related and International Emergency Funding: A Review of Congressional Oversight Issues**\n\nSince the 9/11 terrorist attacks, Congress has appropriated approximately $2 trillion in discretionary budget authority for war-related and other international emergency or contingency-designated activities. This funding has been used to support the U.S. government's response to the 9/11 attacks and other related international affairs activities. The majority of this funding, $1.8 trillion, has been allocated to the Department of Defense (DOD), with $154 billion going to the Department of State and U.S. Agency for International Development (USAID), and $3 billion to the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) and Coast Guard.\n\nThe Overseas Contingency Operations (OCO) fund has been used to finance a wide range of activities, including military operations, foreign aid, and diplomatic efforts. However, the use of OCO funding has been criticized for being ambiguous and for allowing the government to circumvent budget caps. The fund has been used to finance activities unrelated to war, such as the purchase of F-35 fighter jets, and has been described as a \"slush fund\" for military and foreign affairs spending.\n\nThe report highlights the need for greater transparency and oversight of OCO funding. The Congressional Budget Office (CBO) estimates that OCO funding for DOD enduring activities, those that would continue in the absence of overseas conflicts, totals more than $50 billion a year. The report recommends that Congress consider transitioning longer-term OCO expenses to the base budget and increasing budget caps to provide more stable and predictable funding for national security.\n\nThe report also notes that the use of OCO funding has created uncertainty in defense planning and has undermined budget controls. The National Defense Strategy Commission has recommended that Congress eliminate the final two years of caps under the Budget Control Act and gradually integrate OCO spending back into the base Pentagon budget.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the need for greater transparency and oversight of OCO funding and recommends that Congress consider transitioning longer-term OCO expenses to the base budget and increasing budget caps to provide more stable and predictable funding for national security.",
